    slug: "revolver-haftanin-en-iyi-6-yeni-sarkisi-7-24-26",
    title: "The 6 Best New Songs of the Week: Madball, Kim Dracula, Avenged Sevenfold, and More",
    excerpt: "Revolver's weekly roundup this time spans hardcore, metalcore, gospel-metal fusion, and alternative detours. Six tracks, from Avenged Sevenfold's surprising new direction to Misery Index's return after four years.",
    body: "At Revolver, we're always on the hunt for new songs worth headbanging to. This week we've rounded up tracks pulled from hardcore, metalcore, and gospel-metal fusion that have been on constant repeat in our newsroom.\n\n**Avenged Sevenfold – \"REJOICE\"**\nA metalcore fan from the turn of the century, teleported into the neon-peach pool of R&B, rock, and electronic music that fills the band's new 'STATICA' EP, would never guess it's the same band. \"REJOICE\" slides into a groove reminiscent of N.E.R.D. at their filthiest, with M. Shadows delivering an autotuned critique of capitalism while Synyster Gates leans into a Pharrell-style falsetto.\n\n**Kim Dracula – \"Molecular Conversion\"**\nA lot happens on the Tasmanian genre alchemist's first solo song since 2023: traces of A Perfect Circle-flavored alt-metal, gospel soul, and King Crimson-esque saxophone screams. Dracula greets the mic with dark thoughts, wild screams, and a glass-shattering operatic wail.\n\n**Imminence – \"False Light\"**\nThe Swedish band previewed their upcoming album 'Axis Mundi,' described as a \"dialogue between darkness and light, violence and beauty,\" with two tracks. \"False Light\" turns beautiful with Eddie Berg's string touches and soft screams, while concrete-footed drums break its spine.\n\n**Madball – \"Don't MisStep\"**\nThe single from the new album 'Not Your Kingdom' brings old-school New York toughness to the fore. Opening with the lines \"The crown is heavy / You can have it if you want it,\" the track proves the NYHC heavyweights are still in fighting shape with hardcore speed and a bruising breakdown.\n\n**Misery Index – \"Feral Future\"**\nThe first song in four years from the Baltimore death-metal veterans honors the relationship between humanity and the earth. Jason Netherton snarls against the \"unconscious, unmoving masses\" who blindly follow man-made rules, arguing instead that \"the sun, the wind, and the water rule.\"\n\n**Showing Teeth – \"Bad Exchanges\"**\n\"God… it's all bad.\" The week's bleakest but most searing track closes out the list.",
